<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.14.3: Writing an Equation of a Line Parallel to a Given Line</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00298</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.14.3: Writing an Equation of a Line Parallel to a Given Line</md:title>
    <md:uuid>5831ac07-6306-46eb-8f7c-5422688a852b</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (20 minutes)</title>
<para>Students will use knowledge of point-slope form to find the equation of lines given a point and a slope. They will then write each equation in slope-intercept form and explore equations with the same slopes and different \(y\)-intercepts. Being exposed to examples and non-examples will help lead them to the concept of parallel lines that have the same slope.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Allow students to work in pairs to go through each step of the activity. Each student should receive a paper of coordinate grids. Colored pencils and a straight-edge are recommended. When graphing, it is suggested that students graph each line in a different color and label the line with the equation. At the end of the activity, have pairs share their results of each step to the class and what they noticed along the way.</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<title>Activity</title>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the equation of the line using point-slope form: \(m = 2\), \((3,1)\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y − 1 = 2(x − 3)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Rewrite your equation from Question 1 in slope-intercept form.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y = 2x − 5\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the equation of the line using point-slope form: \(m = 2\), \((−1,4)\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y − 4 = 2(x + 1)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Rewrite your equation from Question 3 in slope-intercept form.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y = 2x + 6\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the equation of the line using point-slope form: \(m = −4\), \((2,1)\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y − 1 = −4(x − 2)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>Rewrite your equation from Question 5  in slope-intercept form.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y = −4x + 9\)</para>

<note><para>Parallel lines are lines that have the same slope. Because they have the same slope, they will never touch, or intersect.</para></note>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the equation of the line using point-slope form: \(m = −4\), \((3,−2)\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y + 2 = −4(x − 3)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>Rewrite your equation from Question 7 in slope-intercept form.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y = −4x + 10\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
  <item> Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph your equations from questions 2 and 4 on the same graph using the Desmos tool below. </item>
</list>
<para>When you have finished graphing, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 
  <figure><media alt="Graphs of y = 2x-5 and y = 2x + 6."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/fc1224cb89d5207ff4f6f2fbb206caff4aa85b22"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="10" type="enumerated">
  <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph your equations from questions 6 and 8 on the same graph using the Desmos tool below. </item>
</list>
<para>When you have finished graphing, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 
  <figure><media alt="Graphs of y = -4x+9 and y = -4x + 10."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f5a82ea2ca1d89633fc3c46fb6eaefe77b928424"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="11" type="enumerated">
<item>What is the same about the equations given in questions 2 and 4?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The slopes are the same. They are both 2. </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="12" type="enumerated">
<item>What is the same about the equations given in questions 6 and 8?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The slopes are the same. They are both -4.</para>


<title>1.14.3: Self Check</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>Which equation represents an equation of a line parallel to the line \( y = \frac{1}{2} x - 3 \) that contains the point \( (6, 4) \)?</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Answers</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Feedback</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>\( y = \frac{1}{2}x + 1 \)</entry>
      <entry>That's correct! Check yourself:
        
        The slope of the parallel line is the same as the original line, \( \frac{1}{2} \).
        
        Substitute \( \frac{1}{2} \) for \( m \), \( 6 \) for \( x_1 \), and \( 4 \) for \( y_1 \) in the point-slope form: \( y - y_1 = m(x - x_1) \).
        
        Simplify the equation \( y - 4 = \frac{1}{2}(x - 6) \) to get \( y - 4 =  \frac{1}{2}x - 3 \). </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\( y = \frac{1}{2}x + 4 \)</entry>
      <entry> Incorrect. Let's try again a different way:
        
        The slope of the parallel line is the same as the original line, \( \frac{1}{2} \). The point on the line is \( (6, 4) \). The first coordinate, \( 6 \), is the \( x \)-coordinate. The second coordinate, \( 4 \), is the \( y \)-coordinate.
        
        Substitute \( \frac{1}{2} \) for \( m \), \( 6 \) for \( x_1 \), and \( 4 \) for \( y_1 \) in the point-slope form: \( y - y_1 = m(x - x_1) \).
        
        Simplify the equation \( y - 4 = \frac{1}{2}(x - 6) \) to get \( y - 4 =  \frac{1}{2}x - 3 \). The answer is \( y = \frac{1}{2}x + 1 \). </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\( y = \frac{1}{2}x - 7 \)</entry>
      <entry> Incorrect. Let's try again a different way:
        
        The slope of the parallel line is the same as the original line, \( \frac{1}{2} \).
        
        Substitute \( \frac{1}{2} \) for \( m \), \( 6 \) for \( x_1 \), and \( 4 \) for \( y_1 \) in the point-slope form: \( y - y_1 = m(x - x_1) \).
        
        Simplify the equation \( y - 4 = \frac{1}{2}(x - 6) \) to get \( y - 4 = \frac{1}{2}x - 3 \). Be sure to add \( 4 \) to both sides of the equation to solve for \( y \). The answer is \( y = \frac{1}{2}x + 1 \). </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\( y = \frac{1}{2}x - 2 \)</entry>
      <entry> Incorrect. Let's try again a different way:
        
        The slope of the parallel line is the same as the original line, \( \frac{1}{2} \).
        
        Substitute \( \frac{1}{2} \) for \( m \), \( 6 \) for \( x_1 \), and \( 4 \) for \( y_1 \) in the point-slope form: \( y - y_1 = m(x - x_1) \).
        
        Simplify the equation \( y - 4 = \frac{1}{2}(x - 6) \). Be sure to distribute \( \frac{1}{2} \) to both terms inside the parentheses to get \( y - 4 =  \frac{1}{2}x - 3 \).
        
        The answer is \( y = \frac{1}{2}x + 1 \). </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>


<title>1.14.3: Additional Resources </title>
<para><emphasis><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it. </emphasis></emphasis></para>
<title>Find the Line Parallel to a Given Line</title>
<para>In this section, you will use what you know about writing the equation of a line given the slope and a point to write the equation of parallel lines. The graph shows the equation of the line \( y = 2x - 3. \) The point \( P \) \( (-2, 1) \) is also plotted. You can use the fact that parallel lines have the same slope to find a line that is parallel to line \(m\) and goes through point \( P \) \( (-2, 1) \). </para>
<para><figure><media alt="This figure has a graph of a straight line and a point on the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y-axes run from negative 8 to 8. The line goes through the points (0, negative 3), (1, negative 1), and (2, 1). The point (negative 2, 1) is plotted. The line does not go through the point (negative 2, 1)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7c5e9aa9d7cccfbead0c314eb26b2bd0ab0f22ef"/></media></figure></para>
<para>From the equation, you know the slope of the line is 2. The second line will pass through \( (-2, 1) \) and have slope 2. To graph the line, start at \( (-2, 1) \) and count out the rise and run. The slope is \( \frac{rise}{run}=\frac{2}{1} \). Count out the rise, 2, and run, 1, and plot the point. You can graph the line as shown. Line \( m \) is parallel to line \( l \) and goes through point \( P \) \( (-2, 1) \).</para>
<para><figure><media alt="This figure has a graph of a two straight lines on the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y-axes run from negative 8 to 8. The first line goes through the points (0, negative 3), (1, negative 1), and (2, 1). The points (negative 2, 1) and (negative 1, 3) are plotted. The second line goes through the points (negative 2, 1) and (negative 1, 3)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/1e8ed5a345016b88a6a1d0363ca7fd31843d9198"/></media></figure></para>
<para>To find the equation of a line parallel to a line through a given point algebraically, you can use what you know about finding the equation of a line given the slope and a point.</para>
<para><emphasis>Example</emphasis></para>
<para>Write an equation of a line parallel to \( y = 2x - 3 \) that contains the point \( (-2, 1) \). Write the equation in slope-intercept form.</para>
<para><emphasis>Solution:</emphasis></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Find the slope of the given line
  The line is in slope-intercept form \( y = 2x - 3 \)
\( m=2 \)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Find the slope of the parallel line
  Parallel lines have the same slope
\( m_1 = 2 \)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Identify the point
  The given point is \( (-2, 1) \)
\( (x_1, y_1) = (-2, 1) \)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Substitute values into the point-slope form \( y - y_1 = m(x - x_1) \)
Simplify
\(\begin{array}{rcl}y-y_1&amp;=&amp;(x-x_1)\\y-1&amp;=&amp;(x-(-2))\\y-1&amp;=&amp;(x+2)\\y-1&amp;=&amp;x+4\end{array}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Write the equation in slope-intercept form
\( y = 2x + 5 \)</para>
<para>Look at the graph with the parallel lines shown previously. Does this equation make sense? What is the \(y\)-intercept of the line? What is the slope?</para>
<para>You can use this table as a reference for writing an equation of a line parallel to a given line. </para>
<para>Write the equation in slope-intercept form: \( y = mx+b \).</para>
<para><emphasis>Solution:</emphasis></para>

<note><para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Find the slope of the given line.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Find the slope of the parallel line.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Identify the point.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Substitute the values into the point-slope form: \( y-y_1=m(x-x_1) \).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Simplify.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6 -</emphasis> Write the equation in slope-intercept form: \( y = mx+b \).</para></note>


<title>Try It: Find the Line Parallel to a Given Line</title>
<para>Write an equation of a line parallel to the line \(y = 3x + 1\) that contains the point \( (4,2) \). Write the equation in slope-intercept form.</para>
<para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para>Here is how to find a line parallel to a given line and point.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Find the slope of the given line.
\( m = 3 \)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Find the slope of the parallel line.
\( m_1 = 3 \)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Identify the point.
  \( (x_1, y_1) \)
\( (4, 2) \)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Substitute the values into the point-slope form: \( y-y_1=m(x-x_1) \).
\(\begin{array}{rcl}y-y_1&amp;=&amp;m(x-x_1)\\y-2&amp;=&amp;3(x-4)\end{array}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Simplify.
\( y - 2 = 3x - 12 \)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6 -</emphasis> Write the equation in slope-intercept form: \( y = mx+b \).
\( y = 3x - 10 \)</para>
</section>
</content>
